| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0.200000000000 | 1.000000000000e-01 | 0.600000000000 |
| 1 | 0.200000000000 | 7.500000000000e-02 | 0.500000000000 |
| 2 | 0.200000000000 | 5.625000000000e-02 | 0.425000000000 |
| 3 | 0.200000000000 | 4.218750000000e-02 | 0.368750000000 |
| 9 | 0.200000000000 | 7.508468627930e-03 | 0.230033874512 |
| 19 | 0.200000000000 | 4.228282585245e-04 | 0.201691313034 |
| 39 | 0.200000000000 | 1.340878021552e-06 | 0.200005363512 |
\image(figures/karn_profile.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual clean-sample states follow the geometric-deviation recurrence.)
\alttext(The deviation decays geometrically while the timeout approaches the constant RTT in the educational model.)

Each event uses constant arithmetic and owned scalar storage, with no allocation
or global state. Copies of an estimator evolve independently; concurrent
mutation of one object requires synchronization. The new event-returning methods
and the legacy void methods share exactly one transition implementation, so
diagnostics do not replay or perturb the estimate. Finite overflow is rejected
atomically, rather than retaining an infinite timeout. No dataset is needed for
these controlled protocol-estimator events.

Independent operation-order checks verified 1,000 actual events. Another 10,000
clean/ignored/timeout events matched every frozen original state field exactly.
Native tests cover old-SRTT deviation order, ignored NaN samples, zero samples,
backoff, atomic invalid/overflow rejection and copied snapshots. The shared
ten-report build passed 131 native tests, 38 directly instrumented
address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer tests, 89 compiled public examples,
documentation coverage for all 388 scoped definitions and ten representative
clangd hovers. All four complete C++ report programs executed successfully and
their native figures were visually reviewed. Batch receipts are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.

> Reference

[RFC6298, Computing TCP's Retransmission Timer|https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6298.html],
especially sections2,3,5, describes the update order, sampling ambiguity and
backoff. This report's estimator deliberately omits the RFC's clock-granularity
term, recommended floor and timer-management machinery, as specified above.
